AWS EC2 —
Think of EC2 as: A virtual server in AWS where you can run applications, Docker, Jenkins, monitoring agents, web servers, etc.

A typical DevOps architecture looks like:

                         Internet
                            |
                       Route 53
                            |
                      Application LB
                            |
              +-------------+-------------+
              |                           |
         EC2 Instance 1              EC2 Instance 2
         AZ-1 / Private              AZ-2 / Private
              |                           |
           Docker                       Docker
              |                           |
          Application                 Application
              +-------------+-------------+
                            |
                          RDS

1. What exactly is EC2?
EC2 = Elastic Compute Cloud
It provides resizable compute capacity in AWS.

Instead of buying a physical server:

Traditional:

Buy Server
   ↓
Install OS
   ↓
Configure Network
   ↓
Install Application
   ↓
Maintain Hardware

AWS:

AWS Console / CLI / Terraform
          ↓
       EC2
          ↓
   Choose AMI + Instance Type
          ↓
      Launch Server
Real-time example

Suppose your company has:

Java Spring Boot Application

You could launch:

EC2
 ├── Amazon Linux
 ├── Java
 ├── Application
 └── Port 8080

Users access it through:

http://server-ip:8080

In production, you normally wouldn't expose EC2 directly. You would use:

User
 ↓
Route 53
 ↓
ALB
 ↓
EC2


2. EC2 Components You MUST Know

For interviews, understand these deeply:

EC2
│
├── AMI
├── Instance Type
├── EBS
├── Security Group
├── Key Pair
├── IAM Role
├── VPC
├── Subnet
├── Elastic IP
├── User Data
├── ENI
├── Placement
├── Monitoring
└── Auto Scaling

These are the core pieces.
